{"reference":"Isaiah 20","book":"Isaiah","chapter":20,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A political warning acted out","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Isaiah’s unusual public action belongs to a setting in which reliance on foreign coalitions and military resources is challenged. The chapter presents Egypt and Ethiopia as the hoped-for help, then depicts their humiliation and captivity under Assyria; the final question exposes the resulting fear: if those relied upon cannot stand, where can escape be found? This can be read as making trust in YHWH a political as well as religious issue. Isaiah’s action also fits the book’s varied portrayal of him through speeches, reports, sign actions, and royal narratives.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 20:1-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_020_c6af9447a50428f3bb893003"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}